    <p>
      I'm Danny, founder of Visibility Audits, a measurement practice for how AI systems
      describe your company to the people deciding whether to buy from you.
    </p>

    <h1>Who I am</h1>

    <p>
      I'm Danny Pollack. I was a Product Manager at Meta, building ads measurement
      systems used by millions of advertisers. The hard part was never running the
      campaign. It was proving the campaign caused anything, when there are so many
      variables at play. AI visibility has the same problem. A company that publishes
      content and sees more citations can't always say that the content did it. That's a
      question I find interesting and it's the one I've built my practice around.
    </p>

    <p>
      Separating the change you caused from the change that would have happened anyway
      is what turns a result into a repeatable strategy. Without it, you're guessing
      which work to repeat. With it, you know which levers move your numbers.
    </p>

    <p>The field is called GEO, or generative engine optimization.</p>

    <h2>How I ended up here</h2>

    <p>
      After six years at Meta, I took time out for the birth of my daughter. In the
      months that followed we moved into a new place and bought most of the things a
      first child needs, the kind of decisions you research properly rather than guess
      at. I noticed I'd stopped starting on Google. I was asking ChatGPT which brands to
      look at, and taking the shortlist it gave me seriously.
    </p>

    <p>
      I realised I was putting a strange amount of trust in an AI synthesis, so I
      started looking at what those answers were actually built from. Often the whole
      recommendation rested on three or four pages I'd never have found on my own.
    </p>

    <p>
      The bigger the purchase, the stronger the pattern. High-consideration buying is
      exactly where people want a confident summary before they start and exactly where
      being left off the shortlist costs the most to the companies in question.
    </p>

    <p>
      So I went looking for who was measuring this properly. I found dashboards and
      trackers, but little that could prove whether a company's AI visibility had
      actually changed, or why. I built the practice around the harder question. Anyone
      can make changes and point at a number that went up. I'm trying to show which
      changes did the work, so you can keep doing those and stop doing the rest.
    </p>

    <h2>Who I work with</h2>

    <p>
      Any company whose buyers research before they commit — where the purchase is
      considered rather than impulsive. It works best when you have a real competitor at
      a similar stage, and nobody in-house looking at this yet.
    </p>

    <p>
      I'm taking on a small number of clients while the practice is new, which means the
      work is mine rather than delegated, and the findings from it are what I publish.
      If that sounds like you, tell me your domain and I'll run the first pass.
    </p>

    <p><a href="mailto:danny@visibility-audits.com">danny@visibility-audits.com</a></p>
